-## Measure performance without creating gates
+## Enforce production performance budgets
 
-A performance target is a nonbinding goal for measured speed or elapsed time. Unlike a declared growth bound, it is not a contract. Missing a target does not fail validation.
+Material production operations have owned workloads, complete-work boundaries, approved raw wall-clock budgets, and qualified execution profiles. The [shared budget contract](https://github.com/nashspence/all/blob/29d2af516a91d762094da1da55c71d07d79e095c/docs/performance.md) owns acceptance: all three complete observations must meet their budget. Invalid evidence, incomplete execution, a budget miss, or missing qualification cannot pass.
 
-Use `make profile` as the shared entry point for maintained profiling scenarios. Add new scenarios there rather than creating separate developer-facing commands. Their implementations remain with their owners.
+Budget authority and environment enrollment remain outside the candidate checkout. Candidate code cannot approve its own budget or change the workload boundary. Include required hashing, authorization, encryption, persistence, finalization, cleanup, and resource receiving in the promised lifecycle.
 
-Report verified work, elapsed time, actual rates, and measurement conditions. Compare only against matching measured references. An unavailable comparison is neither a pass nor a failure. Invalid evidence and failed execution remain errors.
+Use `make profile` for maintained scenarios; keep operation adapters with their owners. Report verified work, elapsed time, and measurement conditions. The [measurement rules](scripts/performance_measurement.py) and [profiling tests](tests/unit/test_transfer_profile_script.py) also maintain advisory comparisons against matching references; these do not replace budget acceptance.
 
-The [measurement rules](scripts/performance_measurement.py) and [profiling tests](tests/unit/test_transfer_profile_script.py) define the precise behavior.
-
-In short, **growth bounds are enforced contracts; performance targets are observations, never gates.**
+Each operation owns its durability and recovery design. Choose the simplest implementation meeting its promises and budgets; no universal replay target, checkpoint ratio, or persistence on every yield is required. Preserve protected commitments and test interruption, retry, and resource custody.
 
 ## Account for validation evidence
 
-Checks record their results and timing as they run, without repeating work just to measure it. The [shared qualification definitions](scripts/ci_qualification.py) identify the work and its conditions without replacing contracts owned by individual operations.
+Checks record results and timing as they run, without repeating work just to measure it. The [qualification definitions](scripts/ci_qualification.py) identify work and conditions without replacing operation contracts.
 
-Keep correctness, growth-contract conformance, and elapsed-time observations separate. Correctness failures, growth violations, and invalid required evidence fail validation. A valid timing miss does not.
+Keep correctness, growth conformance, production-budget verdicts, and validation-runtime observations distinct. Correctness failures, growth violations, failed required budgets, and invalid required evidence fail validation. Ordinary test or CI duration targets remain advisory; a valid miss does not block work or require approval.
 
-Report elapsed time even when no numerical target or comparable reference exists. Use credible performance evidence to recommend investigation or improvement. The stronger the evidence and the more serious its likely consequences, the stronger the recommendation may be.
-
-Record significant findings and recommendations in GitHub. Performance targets do not create approval-to-proceed conditions or authorize weakening required tests, workloads, or safety limits.
-
-In short, **validation evidence establishes compliance; performance observations inform judgment.**
+Record elapsed time without requiring a target or comparable reference. Record significant findings in GitHub. Neither performance goals nor budget failures authorize weakening required tests, workloads, or safety limits.
 
